From: Caboose <caboose@local>
Subject: [PATCH 95/99] Send a process's Vulkan start without waiting for its replies

With one-way start (hello 0x1000000, granted with silent success) the process names its
instances, physical devices, devices and queues itself, as it names its other objects. A
vkCreateInstance, vkCreateDevice or vkGetDeviceQueue2 that an identical one succeeded
before in the executor session (the same creation parameters, a device of the same
fingerprint naming the same queue) goes between REPLY_MODE(3) and REPLY_MODE(1), without
its reply: the process
predicts its answer, and a FAULT on the feed when the phone does not bear it out loses the
device, as for silent success. The first of its kind is asked, and its success is kept in
the session's instance scope (caboose-bounded-memory/one_way_start.h), which a new executor
connection starts empty. A process whose feed is not yet running waits briefly for it
before its first such command. Zink asked these about six times at every start.

vkEnumeratePhysicalDevices names the physical devices it lists but always reads the list
back: the phone keeps the handle it first gave a physical device, so a list sent without
its reply could leave the process using handles the phone does not know (an empty
physical device: no queue families, which DXVK then submits to).
